ho are at least&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"10\"><mn>10</mn></math> years of age. Based on the table, of the total number of participants, there are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"30\"><mn>30</mn></math> participants who are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"10\"><mn>10</mn></math>&ndash;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"19\"><mn>19</mn></math> years of age and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"30\"><mn>30</mn></math> participants who are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"20 plus\"><mn>20</mn><mo>+</mo></math> years of age. Therefore, a total of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"30 plus 30\"><mn>30</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>30</mn></math>, or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"60\"><mn>60</mn></math>, of the participants are at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"10\"><mn>10</mn></math> years of age. Thus, the probability of selecting a participant from group A, given that the participant is at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"10\"><mn>10</mn></math> years of age, is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"StartFraction 25 Over 60 EndFraction\"><mfrac><mn>25</mn><mn>60</mn></mfrac></math>, or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"five twelfths\"><mfrac><mn>5</mn><mn>12</mn></mfrac></math>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice A is incorrect. This is the number of participants from group A who are at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"10\"><mn>10</mn></math> years of age divided by the total number of participants, rather than divided by the number of participants who are at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"10\"><mn>10</mn></math> years of age.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice C is incorrect. This is the probability of randomly selecting a participant from group A, given that the participant is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"10\"><mn>10</mn></math>&ndash;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"19\"><mn>19</mn></math> years of age, rather than given that the participant is at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"10\"><mn>10</mn></math> years of age.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice D is incorrect. This is the probability of randomly selecting a participant who is at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"10\"><mn>10</mn></math> years of age, given that the participant is in group A.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"image_count":0,"raw_list":{"updateDate":1755117017725,"pPcc":"SAT#Q","questionId":"89f20d9e","skill_cd":"Q.E.","score_band_range_cd":7,"skill_desc":"Probability and conditional probability","createDate":1755117017725,"program":"SAT","primary_class_cd_desc":"Problem-Solving and Data Analysis","ibn":"","external_id":"edb435d2-fe23-4422-9f29-3461bdaa70a9","primary_class_cd":"Q","uId":"d3cd5898-69cf-4a80-9a67-3f191809b685","difficulty":"H"},"raw_detail":{"type":"mcq","stem":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The table summarizes the distribution of age and assigned group for <math alttext=\"90\"><mn>90</mn>\n</math> participants in a study.</p>\n<figure class=\"table\"><table border=\"1\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"width: 18.0583%;\">&nbsp;</td>\n<th style=\"width: 18.0583%; text-align: center;\" scope=\"col\"><math alttext=\"0\"><mn>0</mn>\n</math>–<math alttext=\"9\"><mn>9</mn>\n</math> years</th>\n<th style=\"width: 18.0583%; text-align: center;\" scope=\"col\"><math alttext=\"10\"><mn>10</mn>\n</math>–<math alttext=\"19\"><mn>19</mn>\n</math> years</th>\n<th style=\"width: 18.0583%; text-align: center;\" scope=\"col\"><math alttext=\"20 plus\"><mn>20</mn><mo>+</mo></math> years</th>\n<th style=\"width: 18.0583%; text-align: center;\" scope=\"col\">Total</th>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th style=\"width: 18.0583%; text-align: left;\" scope=\"row\">Group A</th>\n<td style=\"width: 18.0583%; text-align: center;\"><math alttext=\"5\"><mn>5</mn>\n</math></td>\n<td style=\"width: 18.0583%; text-align: center;\"><math alttext=\"17\"><mn>17</mn>\n</math></td>\n<td style=\"width: 18.0583%; text-align: center;\"><math alttext=\"8\"><mn>8</mn>\n</math></td>\n<td style=\"width: 18.0583%; text-align: center;\"><math alttext=\"30\"><mn>30</mn>\n</math></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th style=\"width: 18.0583%; text-align: left;\" scope=\"row\">Group B</th>\n<td style=\"width: 18.0583%; text-align: center;\"><math alttext=\"6\"><mn>6</mn>\n</math></td>\n<td style=\"width: 18.0583%; text-align: center;\"><math alttext=\"8\"><mn>8</mn>\n</math></td>\n<td style=\"width: 18.0583%; text-align: center;\"><math alttext=\"16\"><mn>16</mn>\n</math></td>\n<td style=\"width: 18.0583%; text-align: center;\"><math alttext=\"30\"><mn>30</mn>\n</math></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th style=\"width: 18.0583%; text-align: left;\" scope=\"row\">Group C</th>\n<td style=\"width: 18.0583%; text-align: center;\"><math alttext=\"19\"><mn>19</mn>\n</math></td>\n<td style=\"width: 18.0583%; text-align: center;\"><math alttext=\"5\"><mn>5</mn>\n</math></td>\n<td style=\"width: 18.0583%; text-align: center;\"><math alttext=\"6\"><mn>6</mn>\n</math></td>\n<td style=\"width: 18.0583%; text-align: center;\"><math alttext=\"30\"><mn>30</mn>\n</math></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th style=\"width: 18.0583%; text-align: center;\" scope=\"row\">Total</th>\n<td style=\"width: 18.0583%; text-align: center;\"><math alttext=\"30\"><mn>30</mn>\n</math></td>\n<td style=\"width: 18.0583%; text-align: center;\"><math alttext=\"30\"><mn>30</mn>\n</math></td>\n<td style=\"width: 18.0583%; text-align: center;\"><math alttext=\"30\"><mn>30</mn>\n</math></td>\n<td style=\"width: 18.0583%; text-align: center;\"><math alttext=\"90\"><mn>90</mn>\n</math></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></figure>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">One of these participants will be selected at random. What is the probability of selecting a participant from group A, given that the participant is at least 10 years of age?</p>","keys":["259b5243-9745-45ce-8068-903e4e193d44"],"answerOptions":[{"id":"03edd3fd-773b-4956-aa2d-e94521d089d7","content":"<p><math alttext=\"five eighteenths\"><mfrac>\n\t<mn>5</mn>\n\t<mn>18</mn>\n</mfrac>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"259b5243-9745-45ce-8068-903e4e193d44","content":"<p><math alttext=\"five twelfths\"><mfrac>\n\t<mn>5</mn>\n\t<mn>12</mn>\n</mfrac>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"af601e26-46c4-4acf-bf24-6de5cbf8a6e0","content":"<p><math alttext=\"StartFraction 17 Over 30 EndFraction\"><mfrac>\n\t<mn>17</mn>\n\t<mn>30</mn>\n</mfrac>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"9b7faf67-be5d-40b8-9fd4-8114be71ac45","content":"<p><math alttext=\"five sixths\"><mfrac>\n\t<mn>5</mn>\n\t<mn>6</mn>\n</mfrac>\n</math></p>"}],"rationale":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice B is correct. Since the participant will be selected at random, the probability of selecting a participant from group A, given that the participant is at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"10\"><mn>10</mn></math>&nbsp;years of age, is equal to the number of participants from group A who are at least&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"10\"><mn>10</mn></math>&nbsp;years of age divided by the total number of participants who are at least&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"10\"><mn>10</mn></math> years of age. Based on the table, in group A, there are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"17\"><mn>17</mn></math>&nbsp;participants who are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"10\"><mn>10</mn></math>&ndash;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"19\"><mn>19</mn></math> years of age and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"8\"><mn>8</mn></math> participants who are&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"20 plus\"><mn>20</mn><mo>+</mo></math>&nbsp;years of age. Therefore, there are a total of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"17 plus 8\"><mn>17</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>8</mn></math>, or&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"25\"><mn>25</mn></math>, participants in group A who are at least&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"10\"><mn>10</mn></math> years of age. Based on the table, of the total number of participants, there are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"30\"><mn>30</mn></math> participants who are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"10\"><mn>10</mn></math>&ndash;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"19\"><mn>19</mn></math> years of age and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"30\"><mn>30</mn></math> participants who are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"20 plus\"><mn>20</mn><mo>+</mo></math> years of age. Therefore, a total of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"30 plus 30\"><mn>30</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>30</mn></math>, or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"60\"><mn>60</mn></math>, of the participants are at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"10\"><mn>10</mn></math> years of age. Thus, the probability of selecting a participant from group A, given that the participant is at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"10\"><mn>10</mn></math> years of age, is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"StartFraction 25 Over 60 EndFraction\"><mfrac><mn>25</mn><mn>60</mn></mfrac></math>, or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"five twelfths\"><mfrac><mn>5</mn><mn>12</mn></mfrac></math>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice A is incorrect. This is the number of participants from group A who are at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"10\"><mn>10</mn></math> years of age divided by the total number of participants, rather than divided by the number of participants who are at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"10\"><mn>10</mn></math> years of age.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice C is incorrect. This is the probability of randomly selecting a participant from group A, given that the participant is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"10\"><mn>10</mn></math>&ndash;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"19\"><mn>19</mn></math> years of age, rather than given that the participant is at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"10\"><mn>10</mn></math> years of age.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice D is incorrect. This is the probability of randomly selecting a participant who is at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"10\"><mn>10</mn></math> years of age, given that the participant is in group A.</p>","externalid":"edb435d2-fe23-4422-9f29-3461bdaa70a9","correct_answer":["B"]},"createDate":1755117017725,"updateDate":1755117017725}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1755117017725, 1755117017725),
    ($SATQ$89f8d08a$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$027102-DC$SATQ$, $SATQ$disclosed$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$SATQ$, $SATQ$Q$SATQ$, $SATQ$Q.F.$SATQ$, $SATQ$Inference from sample statistics and margin of error $SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$<p class="stem_paragraph ">A store manager reviewed the receipts from 80&nbsp;customers who were selected at random from all the customers who made purchases last Thursday. Of those selected, 20&nbsp;receipts showed that the customer had purchased fruit. If 1,500&nbsp;customers made purchases last Thursday, which of the following is the most appropriate conclusion?</p>
$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"a","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">Exactly 75 customers must have purchased fruit last Thursday.</p>\n"},{"id":"b","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">Exactly 375&nbsp;customers must have purchased fruit last Thursday.</p>\n"},{"id":"c","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">The best estimate for the number of customers who purchased fruit last Thursday is&nbsp;75.</p>\n"},{"id":"d","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">The best estimate for the number of customers who purchased fruit last Thursday is&nbsp;375.</p>\n"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, NULL, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is correct. It&rsquo;s given that the manager took a random selection of the receipts of 80 customers from a total of 1,500. It&rsquo;s also given that of those 80 receipts, 20 showed that the customer had purchased fruit. This means that an appropriate estimate of the fraction of customers who purchased fruit is <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/89f8d08a/img_000_83446d82.png" alt="the fraction 20 over 80"></span>, or <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/89f8d08a/img_001_929b687b.png" alt="one fourth "></span>. Multiplying this fraction by the total number of customers yields <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/89f8d08a/img_002_3806cd31.png" alt="one fourth times 1,500, equals 375"></span>. Therefore, the best estimate for the number of customers who purchased fruit is 375.<p>Choices A and B are incorrect because an exact number of customers can&rsquo;t be known from taking a random selection. Additionally, choice A may also be the result of a calculation error. Choice C is incorrect and may result from a calculation error.&nbsp;</p><p>&nbsp;</p></p>
$SATQ$, false, 3, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$Multiple Choice$SATQ$, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ${"questionId":"89f8d08a","external_id":null,"disclosed_item_id":"027102-DC","source":"disclosed","program":"SAT","test_type":2,"test_type_desc":"Math","primary_class_cd":"Q","domain":"Problem-Solving and Data Analysis","skill_cd":"Q.F.","skill_desc":"Inference from sample statistics and margin of error ","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":null,"stem":"<p class=\"stem_paragraph \">A store manager reviewed the receipts from 80&nbsp;customers who were selected at random from all the customers who made purchases last Thursday. Of those selected, 20&nbsp;receipts showed that the customer had purchased fruit. If 1,500&nbsp;customers made purchases last Thursday, which of the following is the most appropriate conclusion?</p>\n","answerOptions":[{"id":"a","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">Exactly 75 customers must have purchased fruit last Thursday.</p>\n"},{"id":"b","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">Exactly 375&nbsp;customers must have purchased fruit last Thursday.</p>\n"},{"id":"c","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">The best estimate for the number of customers who purchased fruit last Thursday is&nbsp;75.</p>\n"},{"id":"d","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">The best estimate for the number of customers who purchased fruit last Thursday is&nbsp;375.</p>\n"}],"keys":null,"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is correct. It&rsquo;s given that the manager took a random selection of the receipts of 80 customers from a total of 1,500. It&rsquo;s also given that of those 80 receipts, 20 showed that the customer had purchased fruit. This means that an appropriate estimate of the fraction of customers who purchased fruit is <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/89f8d08a/img_000_83446d82.png\" alt=\"the fraction 20 over 80\"></span>, or <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/89f8d08a/img_001_929b687b.png\" alt=\"one fourth \"></span>. Multiplying this fraction by the total number of customers yields <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/89f8d08a/img_002_3806cd31.png\" alt=\"one fourth times 1,500, equals 375\"></span>. Therefore, the best estimate for the number of customers who purchased fruit is 375.<p>Choices A and B are incorrect because an exact number of customers can&rsquo;t be known from taking a random selection. Additionally, choice A may also be the result of a calculation error. Choice C is incorrect and may result from a calculation error.&nbsp;</p><p>&nbsp;</p></p>\n","answer_style":"Multiple Choice","section":"Math","image_count":3,"raw_list":{"updateDate":1691007959621,"pPcc":"SAT#Q","questionId":"89f8d08a","skill_cd":"Q.F.","score_band_range_cd":4,"skill_desc":"Inference from sample statistics and margin of error ","createDate":1691007959621,"program":"SAT","primary_class_cd_desc":"Problem-Solving and Data Analysis","ibn":"027102-DC","external_id":null,"primary_class_cd":"Q","uId":"db7c9f81-d98d-44bc-aeb5-0e66cd41857e","difficulty":"M"},"raw_detail":{"item_id":"027102-DC","section":"Math","prompt":"<p class=\"stem_paragraph \">A store manager reviewed the receipts from 80&nbsp;customers who were selected at random from all the customers who made purchases last Thursday. Of those selected, 20&nbsp;receipts showed that the customer had purchased fruit. If 1,500&nbsp;customers made purchases last Thursday, which of the following is the most appropriate conclusion?</p>\n","answer":{"style":"Multiple Choice","choices":{"a":{"body":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">Exactly 75 customers must have purchased fruit last Thursday.</p>\n"},"b":{"body":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">Exactly 375&nbsp;customers must have purchased fruit last Thursday.</p>\n"},"c":{"body":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">The best estimate for the number of customers who purchased fruit last Thursday is&nbsp;75.</p>\n"},"d":{"body":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">The best estimate for the number of customers who purchased fruit last Thursday is&nbsp;375.</p>\n"}},"correct_choice":"d","rationale":"<p>Choice D is correct. It&rsquo;s given that the manager took a random selection of the receipts of 80 customers from a total of 1,500. It&rsquo;s also given that of those 80 receipts, 20 showed that the customer had purchased fruit. This means that an appropriate estimate of the fraction of customers who purchased fruit is <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"the fraction 20 over 80\"></span>, or <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"one fourth \"></span>. Multiplying this fraction by the total number of customers yields <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"one fourth times 1,500, equals 375\"></span>. Therefore, the best estimate for the number of customers who purchased fruit is 375.<p>Choices A and B are incorrect because an exact number of customers can&rsquo;t be known from taking a random selection. Additionally, choice A may also be the result of a calculation error. Choice C is incorrect and may result from a calculation error.&nbsp;</p><p>&nbsp;</p></p>\n"}},"createDate":1691007959621,"updateDate":1691007959621}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959621, 1691007959621),
    ($SATQ$8a714fa1$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$026407-DC$SATQ$, $SATQ$disclosed$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$SATQ$, $SATQ$Q$SATQ$, $SATQ$Q.B.$SATQ$, $SATQ$Percentages$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$<p class="stem_paragraph ">Which of the following represents the result of increasing the quantity&nbsp;<span class="italic">x</span> by 9%, where <span class="math_expression "><span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/8a714fa1/img_004_8d906565.png" alt="x is greater than 0"></span></span> ?</p>
$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"a","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \"><span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/8a714fa1/img_000_77cfc2d6.png\" alt=\"1 point 0 9 x\"></span></span></p>\n"},{"id":"b","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \"><span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/8a714fa1/img_001_b035eadc.png\" alt=\"0 point 0 9 x\"></span></span></p>\n"},{"id":"c","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \"><span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/8a714fa1/img_002_e49fa49a.png\" alt=\"x plus 9\"></span></span></p>\n"},{"id":"d","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \"><span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/8a714fa1/img_003_630123df.png\" alt=\"x plus 0 point 0 9\"></span></span></p>\n"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, NULL, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is correct. Increasing the positive quantity <span class="italic">x</span> by 9% is the result of adding 9% of <span class="italic">x</span> to <span class="italic">x</span>. 9% of <span class="italic">x</span> can be represented algebraically as <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/8a714fa1/img_005_f4fd4afc.png" alt="the fraction, 9 over 100, end fraction, times x"></span>, or <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/8a714fa1/img_006_358da529.png" alt="0 point 0 9 x"></span>. Adding this expression to <span class="italic">x</span> yields <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/8a714fa1/img_007_b7c821ca.png" alt="x plus 0 point 0 9 x"></span>, or <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/8a714fa1/img_008_b5a0009b.png" alt="1 point 0 9 x"></span>.<p>Choice B is incorrect. This represents 9% of <span class="italic">x</span>. Choice C is incorrect. This represents increasing <span class="italic">x</span> by 9, not by 9%. Choice D is incorrect. This represents increasing <span class="italic">x</span> by 0.09, not by 9%.</p><p>&nbsp;</p></p>
